'''bilberry''' (bil′ber″i), ''n.''; pl. ''bilberries'' (-iz). [Formerly also spelled ''bill-berry'' and ''bull-berry''. The last form, if not simulated, is prob. right, < ''bull''<sup>1</sup> + ''berry''<sup>1</sup>. Another species, the red whortleberry, is named ''cowberry'', and the NL. name of the genus, ''Vaccinium'', means ‘cow-berry.’ The word ''bull'' enters into the names of several other plants, as ''bullweed, bullwort, bulrush''. Cf. ''hartberry'', another name for ''bilberry''. But the relation of the equiv. Dan. ''bölle-bær'', also simply ''bölle'', whortleberry, to Dan. ''böll'', a castrated bull (cf. Icel. ''boli'' = Norw. ''bol'' = E. ''bull''<sup>1</sup>), is not clear. The usual Dan. term for bull is ''tyr'' = Sw. ''tjur'' = Icel. ''stjörr'' = E. ''steer''. The name ''blaeberry'' is of different origin: see ''blaeberry''.] '''1.''' A shrub and its fruit, ''Vaccinium Myrtillus''. {{smaller|In Scotland the bilberry is usually called ''blaeberry'', from its ''blae'' or dark-blue color. See ''Vaccinium'' and ''whortleberry''.}}

'''2.''' A name sometimes given in the United States to the fruit of the shad-bush, ''Amelanchier Canadensis''.—'''Bog-bilberry''', {{smaller|''Vaccinium uliginosum'' of the United States and Europe.}}—'''Dwarf bilberry''', {{smaller|''V. cæspitosum''.}}—'''Jamaica bilberry''', {{smaller|''V. meridionale''.}}

'''bilbo'''<sup>1</sup> (bil′bō), ''n.''; pl. ''bilboes'' or ''-bos'' (-bōz). [Early mod. E. also ''bilbow, bilboe, bilboa'', prop. a sword of ''Bilbao'' (in E. formerly ''Bilboa'') in

readings.c2s1:
Spain, such swords being, like those of Toledo (see ''Toledo''), held in high esteem for their temper.] '''1.''' Formerly, a sword or sword-blade, famous for extreme elasticity, made in Bilbao in Spain.
{{smaller block|Compass’d like a good ''bilbo'' in the circumference of a peck, hilt to point, heel to head.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., iii. 5.}}}}
Hence—'''2.''' Any sword. [Poetical.]
{{smaller block|At Poitiers bath’d their ''bilboes'' in French blood.
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, xvi. 72.}}}}

'''bilbo<sup>2</sup>''' (bil′bō), ''n.''; pl. ''bilboes'' or ''-bos'' (-bōz). [Early mod. E. also ''bilbow'', ''bilboe'', usually in pl.; prob. so named, like ''bilbo''<sup>1</sup>, from ''Bilbao'' in Spain; but direct evidence is lacking.] A
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Bilboes, from the Tower of London.}}}}
long bar or bolt of iron having sliding shackles and a lock, formerly used to confine the feet of prisoners or offenders, especially on board ship: usually in the plural.
{{smaller block|<poem>Methought I lay
Worse than the mutines in the ''bilboes''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, v. 2.}}}}

'''bilbo-man†''' (bil′bō-man), ''n.'' A swordsman.
{{smaller block|<poem>You are much bound to your ''bilbo-men'';
I am glad you are straight again, captain.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', King and No King, v. 3.}}}}

